.\" Hand-written. design/tpm-unlock.md ("Provisioning", "Supported releases", "--portable", "--server", "The acceptance record") is the full account.
.TH iron-vault-provision-host-unlock 8 "" "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-provision\-host\-unlock \- seal a host unlock identity to this machine's TPM
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault\-provision\-host\-unlock\fR \fB\-\-server\fR [\fB\-\-accept\-physical\-access\fR]
.br
\fBiron\-vault\-provision\-host\-unlock\fR \fB\-\-portable\fR [\fB\-\-accept\-no\-second\-secret\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Generates a host unlock identity with \fBage\-keygen\fR(1) and seals it to the TPM with \fBsystemd\-creds\fR(1),
bound to PCR 7, as \fI/etc/iron\-vault\-host/host\-unlock.cred\fR.
At each boot \fIiron\-vault\-host\-unlock.service\fR unseals it as root and hands it to \fIiron\-vault.service\fR,
which then unlocks the vault with no one present.
The service itself never opens the TPM.
.PP
The secret text exists only in pipes.
Of the identity, only its recipient key is printed, \fBage1\fR..., to enrol with \fBiron\-vault init \-\-host\-unlock\fR or
\fBiron\-vault recipient add \-\-host\-unlock\fR.
The vault passphrase and the offline recovery key keep working: if the unseal ever fails, the service starts Locked
and waits for \fBiron\-vault unlock\fR.
.PP
Run it once, as root.
It refuses to run when the blob already exists; move the old one aside first.
It never clears the TPM, takes ownership, sets hierarchy authorization or changes dictionary\-attack parameters.
.PP
TPM unlock is supported on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS only, and the helper stops on any other release.
.SH OPTIONS
One of \fB\-\-server\fR and \fB\-\-portable\fR is required; there is no default.
.TP
\fB\-\-server\fR
For a server or appliance.
Anyone with physical access to the machine and a trusted boot image can start the vault.
The helper says so and seals nothing until you type \fByes\fR at a terminal.
It never reads the answer from a pipe.
.TP
\fB\-\-accept\-physical\-access\fR
With \fB\-\-server\fR, accepts that condition for a run whose standard input is not a terminal.
At a terminal the helper still asks for \fByes\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-portable\fR
For a laptop or other portable machine, which needs a full\-disk\-encryption pre\-boot secret as well: a LUKS
passphrase typed at boot, or a LUKS TPM key slot that needs a PIN.
The helper checks once, now, how the disk holding \fI/etc/iron\-vault\-host\fR is unlocked.
On a btrfs or bcachefs filesystem it checks every device of the filesystem, not only the one \fBfindmnt\fR names.
It warns when any part of the disk is not encrypted, when a \fBsystemd\-tpm2\fR token needs no PIN, when a \fBclevis\fR
token or LUKS1 \fBluksmeta\fR binding exists, when a token is of a type it does not recognise,
when \fI/etc/crypttab\fR names a key file for the device or has \fBtpm2\-device=\fR beside a PIN\-less token, and when
any \fI/boot/initrd.img\-*\fR holds a key file.
A check that cannot run, for example for want of \fBcryptsetup\fR, \fBluksmeta\fR or \fBlsinitramfs\fR, also warns, and says
how to fix that; it never passes.
Each warning says that anyone who steals the machine can boot it and start the vault, and names the fix: encrypt the
disk, remove the key file, or re\-enrol the slot with \fBsystemd\-cryptenroll \-\-tpm2\-with\-pin=yes\fR, which changes the
key slot, not the data.
With any warning the helper seals nothing until you type \fByes\fR at a terminal; it never reads the answer from a pipe.
The check does not see a key slot added later: run the helper again after any change to the disk's key slots.
.TP
\fB\-\-accept\-no\-second\-secret\fR
With \fB\-\-portable\fR, accepts the warnings for a run whose standard input is not a terminal.
At a terminal the helper still asks for \fByes\fR.
.SH ACCEPTANCE RECORD
Before it seals, for both modes, the helper records the mode, each warning, how the risk was accepted (\fBterminal\fR or
\fBflag\fR) and \fBSUDO_USER\fR.
The journal gets one entry per line through \fBlogger\fR(1), tag \fIiron\-vault\-host\-unlock\fR, at \fBauth.warning\fR
when a warning was accepted and \fBauth.notice\fR otherwise.
\fI/etc/iron\-vault\-host/provisioning.txt\fR holds the same lines with the time, and is replaced whole at each run.
If either cannot be written the helper stops without sealing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the identity is sealed, 1 when the helper stopped, 2 for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI/etc/iron\-vault\-host/host\-unlock.cred\fR
The host unlock blob: root, mode 0600, in a root 0700 directory.
.TP
\fI/etc/iron\-vault\-host/provisioning.txt\fR
The acceptance record: root, mode 0600.
.TP
\fI/run/iron\-vault\-unlock/host\-unlock\fR
The unlock handoff, on a ramfs, present only while the handoff unit is active.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBiron\-vault\fR(1), \fBiron\-vault\-init\fR(1), \fBiron\-vault\-recipient\-add\fR(1), \fBsystemd\-creds\fR(1)
